## MPI on the Head Node

The {{ provisioner_name }} services run in podman networks, so the head node
carries their bridge interfaces (`podman0`, `podman1`, ...) next to its real
ones. libfabric's *tcp* provider enumerates every interface that is up, and
MPICH can pick one of the bridges as its default NIC. An MPI program started
on the head node then fails with an error such as:

```
OFI call tsenddata failed (default nic=podman3: No such file or directory)
```

Jobs run through the resource manager are not affected, because the compute
nodes carry no podman interfaces. Running MPI programs on the head node
itself is unusual; the OpenHPC test suite does it, and pins libfabric to the
loopback interface for exactly those tests. For an interactive MPI run on
the head node, pin libfabric's *tcp* provider to an interface for that
session, for example:

```bash
export FI_TCP_IFACE=${sms_eth_internal}
```

::: {.tip}
**Note:** job launchers forward the environment, so `FI_TCP_IFACE` set in a
login shell also reaches the jobs submitted from it. Do not set it
permanently, for example in `/etc/profile.d`, unless the compute nodes'
internal interface has the same name as the head node's.
:::
